verdict.reasoning:
I could not refute the claim. edits.effective (rsi6/open/edits.py:187-196) sets to 0.0 every theta entry that no live score or pick instruction reads. The Acquirer, however, starts from the whole theta: A[0] = A[3] = start and C = tile(start) (design_o.py:587-589). Its A-programs can contain 'mean', which returns np.full(d, x.mean()), and 'norm', which returns x/||x||*sqrt(d) (design_o.py:515-519). Both are in A_UNARY, so they are in A_OPS and in PARTS_OPS for init, propose and update (design_o.py:72, 79), and edits.random_instruction draws from PARTS_OPS (edits.py ~54). Once such an op is present, the values of unread constants change the step sizes and proposals on the dimensions that are read.

discovery.py keys three things by this behaviour key:
- the 'seen' set (lines 108, 131-132), so a mechanism that really is different is thrown away as a duplicate;
- the memo of (score, cpu) (lines 111-115, 138);
- the acceptance measurement (line 205).

run_dev.py:63-66 passes one memo dict to all lineages, and migration merges 'seen' sets (discovery.py:242). So one lineage can be handed another lineage's measured value for a mechanism that behaves differently.

The collision needs two things together:
- a cross-coordinate op (mean or norm) in an A-program;
- an unread constant, which appears when score or pick instructions become dead, or when a constant is grown or perturbed into a dead slot.

Generation 0 has neither, so the collisions are reachable through edits but are not there from the start. Without mean or norm I found the zeroing sound: the proposals on the read dimensions were identical.
